# Mission Control project conventions

These evolve. Changing one gets a dated entry in `docs/decisions/decision-log.md`.

## Repository layout

`/Users/p92654/Desktop/Intake-Agent-Practice` is the single project root.

```
.cursor/rules/    aaa-company-guardrails.mdc, intake-project.mdc, uxui-architect.mdc
.cursor/skills/   verify-live-ux, unrestricted-research
.cursor/plans/    canonical working plans
assets/brand/     style guide PDF, EPS print masters, web logo derivatives
assets/mocks/     UI screens, flow diagrams, the Workfront form example
assets/exports/   generated briefs and screenshots
docs/INDEX.md     manifest of every document
docs/knowledge/   requirements, flows, brand system, A3 schema
docs/briefs/      brief templates and real AAA Life brief artifacts
docs/decisions/   decision log
docs/audits/      AAA Life UX audits
docs/plans/       date-stamped snapshots of superseded plans
src/              app code
```

## Naming and formats

- **No colons or spaces in file or folder names.** macOS Finder renders a colon as a slash, so
  `knowledge:` looked normal in Finder while being invisible to directory listings - the brand PDF and
  every design asset were unreachable by pattern and only readable by exact path. That is the failure
  this rule prevents.
- **Markdown for all new documentation. No new RTF.** RTF control codes cannot be diffed.
- Canonical plans live in `.cursor/plans/`. Snapshots in `docs/plans/` are date-stamped so two
  versions never compete silently.
- Never delete a source file before its replacement is verified. Converted originals move to
  `docs/knowledge/source-originals/`.

## Storage

- **`aaa-project-tracker` is the deliverables tracker's `localStorage` key. Never rename it without a
  migration.** It holds real user data with no server copy.
- Intake storage mirrors `src/lib/storage.ts`: a primary key plus a backup key, quota messaging, and
  a repair-not-reject parse. A single bad value must never cost the user a record, let alone all of
  them.
- All data is per-browser. There is no server copy of anything.

## Workfront discipline

- **The app never invents a Workfront field.** Anything submitted must map to a field that exists on
  the A3 Creative Intake custom form; see `docs/knowledge/a3-schema.md`.
- Data with no home in the schema is written into the Issue name or description as a labeled audit
  line, and raised as a **Workfront configuration request**. The ALAN number and match coverage are
  both currently in this state.
- Internal field names (`A3Intake*`) are the contract. Do not rename them to something friendlier.
- `submitToWorkfront` is a local stub. It makes no network call and always reports `local`.

## Integration pattern

Every external system enters as a **typed adapter behind an interface**, with a deterministic local
implementation shipping first:

| Interface | Sprint 1 implementation | Later |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `IntakeAssistant` | `TemplateAssistant` | `LlmAssistant` behind a server proxy |
| `RelatedWorkMatcher` | `LocalMatcher` | `WorkfrontMatcher`, semantic matcher |
| `AlanNumberDetector` | `NoopDetector` | `PdfAlanDetector` |

This is why the matcher takes its corpus as an argument instead of reaching for storage itself: a
Workfront-backed corpus becomes a swap, not a rewrite.

## Hosting portability

The app must stay deployable to any static host without code changes.

- The base path comes from the `APP_BASE_PATH` build variable and reaches the router through
  `import.meta.env.BASE_URL`. Never hard-code an absolute asset or route path.
- Client-side routes are not real files, so every host needs an SPA history fallback. Configs for
  Vercel, Netlify, Azure Static Web Apps, and IIS are maintained together - update all of them or
  none. See `docs/deployment.md`.
- No secret in a `VITE_`-prefixed variable, ever. Those are compiled into the public bundle.
- Keep the app free of third-party runtime requests so a strict Content-Security-Policy stays
  achievable. Self-host fonts and assets.

## Session-start documentation scan

At the start of each planning or build session:

1. Scan `docs/` and `assets/`, newest first, **by explicit path as well as by pattern**. A listing can
   miss files that are really there; this has already happened once.
2. Diff against `docs/INDEX.md`. Anything unlisted is new.
3. Read what is new, summarize it in a sentence or two, and state plainly whether it changes scope, a
   guardrail, or nothing.
4. If it changes scope or a guardrail, **propose the edit and wait for confirmation**. New
   documentation never silently rewrites an approved plan.
5. Update `docs/INDEX.md`, and append to `docs/decisions/decision-log.md` when a decision resulted.

Guardrails are **append-and-supersede**: a changed guardrail gets a new dated entry marking the prior
one superseded, so the reasoning stays auditable for Compliance and for whoever inherits this.
